  "provider_query": "Public Telegram web preview of the official Telegram channel",
  "answer": "The official Telegram channel is **@telegram** (“Telegram News”). Its public web preview (no app or login) is:\n\n**https://t.me/s/telegram**\n\nChannel link (app/join prompt): **https://t.me/telegram**\n\nAbout text on the preview: “The official Telegram on Telegram.” Subscriber counts shown on recent previews are on the order of ~9–11.5M, depending on the snapshot.\n\n### Relevant results\n\n1. **Public preview of the official channel** — https://t.me/s/telegram — recent posts, subscriber count, and media via Telegram’s `/s/` HTML preview. [web:8]\n2. **Same channel, older page snapshot** — https://t.me/s/telegram/81 — same @telegram / “Telegram News” identity and description. [web:12]\n3. **How the public preview works** — public channels expose a browser view at `t.me/s/<channel>` (about the last ~20 posts as HTML; no account). [web:1] [web:2]\n4. **Paging older posts** — add `?before=<post-id>` on the preview URL (example pattern: `t.me/s/telegram?before=…`). [web:2] [web:8]\n5. **Third-party browser layer** — https://tg.me/ describes opening public `t.me` links in the browser (e.g. `t.me/name` → `tg.me/name`). [web:0]\n6. **Other public viewers** — tools such as OpenTG and AnonyTG only resolve **public** channels/posts and link back to Telegram as the source. [web:7] [web:4]\n7. **Official announcements policy** — the channel states official announcements are published at telegram.org; other sites claiming to speak for the team are likely scams. [web:12]\n8. **Not the official channel** — similarly named or “official” third-party channels (e.g. unrelated “Official channel” pages) are separate from @telegram. [web:9] [web:10]\n\nUse **https://t.me/s/telegram** for the public web preview of Telegram’s own channel.",
